WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.320
Model quantization represents weights, and sometimes activations or caches,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.720
with lower numerical precision.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.440
A model originally stored with 32-bit or 16-bit floating-point values may be converted so

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.745
many weights use 8,

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:13.585
6, 5, 4, or fewer bits.

00:00:13.585 --> 00:00:17.760
The runtime combines those compact values with scales and other metadata to approximate

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.200
the original computations during inference.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.200
Lower precision reduces the bytes that must be stored, loaded, and moved through memory.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.180
That can make a model fit in limited VRAM or system RAM and reduce memory-bandwidth

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.540
pressure during token generation.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.900
A model that remains fully on an accelerator may run much faster than a higher-precision

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.320
version split between GPU and CPU.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.720
Smaller files also download and load more quickly.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.720
The tradeoff is approximation.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.950
Quantization can alter probabilities and model behavior,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.480
with effects that depend on architecture, method, bit level, and task.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.880
Some models tolerate moderate quantization well;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.395
aggressive compression may damage reasoning, coding, instruction following,

00:00:52.395 --> 00:00:53.580
or rare knowledge.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.810
Hardware and runtimes also differ in which formats they accelerate efficiently.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.215
Quantization is not magic compression.

00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:03.120
It does not remove context-cache memory, runtime overhead, tokenizer files,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.200
or every activation.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.670
It does not increase the model's underlying capability or guarantee speed.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.800
The useful question is whether a particular quantized artifact fits the target system,

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.880
runs efficiently in the selected runtime,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.280
and preserves acceptable quality for the real workload.
